@*
    The frame every screen sits in.

    FIVE ITEMS, and the middle one is not a destination.

    Home, Services, Voice, You, Settings. Voice is the mark itself, raised into the
    middle of the bar where a thumb rests, because talking is not a screen you go
    to - it is what you do from wherever you already are. That is also why an
    earlier bar with "Talk" and "Type" as separate items was the wrong shape: one
    conversation, two ways in, reachable everywhere.

    What is NOT here is as decided as what is. Documents and Language are settings
    of a particular SERVICE rather than places: somebody can use the app in
    English, want their CV in Japanese, and want translating between Korean and
    Mandarin. Three language settings, each belonging to the service it configures,
    none of them global and none of them a tab.
*@
@implements IDisposable
@inherits LayoutComponentBase
@inject NavigationManager Nav
@inject IConversation Talk
@inject IShareTarget Share
@inject VoiceMark Mark
@inject IResidentAssistant Resident
@inject CapabilityRegistry Can

<div class="app">
    @*
        NO TOP BAR ON HOME. It carries the mark and a way home - and the bottom bar
        now has both, so on Home it was the same two things twice while taking a
        fifth of the screen from the circle that is the point of it. Everywhere
        else it says WHERE YOU ARE, which the bottom bar cannot.
    *@
    @if (ScreenName is not null)
    {
        <header class="app-bar" role="banner">
            <div class="bar-home" role="button" tabindex="0" @onclick="GoHome">
                <BrandMark Size="30" Ring="true" Decorative="true" />
                <span class="app-name">Circle AI</span>
            </div>
            <span class="app-where">@ScreenName</span>
        </header>
    }


    @*
        WHAT THE APP IS DOING, ON THE GLASS.

        THE MODE USED TO BE INVISIBLE. It lived four taps into Settings, and it
        silently repointed a link on Home labelled "Or type instead" so that one
        control meant two different screens. Translate - the thing on the front
        of the app - appeared in no menu at all; the only way in was to know that
        the mode existed and go and find it.

        Three peers you switch between, not a hierarchy you climb, so this stays
        put and stays lit rather than changing as you go deeper. It is what the
        circle will do, which is why it sits above the circle and not in a bar of
        places to go: the bottom bar is WHERE YOU GO, this is WHAT IT DOES.

        Hidden on the full-screen stages for the same reason the bottom bar is -
        loading and setup own the whole screen, and offering a mode before there
        is a model to run it with is offering something that cannot happen.
    *@
    @if (!IsFullScreen)
    {
        @* On Home there is no bar above this, so the strip is the topmost thing
           on the screen and has to clear the status bar itself - see the note on
           .modestrip-top. Everywhere else the app-bar has already done it. *@
        <nav class="modestrip @(ScreenName is null ? "modestrip-top" : null)"
             role="tablist" aria-label="What it does">
            @foreach (var mode in AppRoutes.Modes)
            {
                <a class="modestrip-item @(IsHere(mode.Route) ? "modestrip-on" : null)"
                   role="tab" aria-selected="@(IsHere(mode.Route) ? "true" : "false")"
                   href="@mode.Route">@mode.OnStrip</a>
            }
        </nav>
    }

    <main class="app-body @(ScreenName is null ? "app-body-flush" : null)">
        @Body
    </main>

    @*
        Five, not eight. More than five gives each item a target too narrow to hit
        on a 344px phone, and the screens that are not here are reached from the
        ones that are: what it can do from the circle, the wake screen from what it
        can do, aiming at a job from the CV.
    *@
    @*
        WHAT THE VOICE BUTTON HAS TO SAY, above the bar it belongs to.

        A layout has no page to put a message on, which is why this button used to
        answer by navigating somewhere that did - and taking somebody off the
        screen they were on, mid-permission-prompt, to read one sentence.
    *@
    @if (_says is { Length: > 0 })
    {
        <div class="says" role="status" @onclick="() => _says = null">@_says</div>
    }

    @*
        NO BAR ON THE FULL-SCREEN STAGES. Loading and setup each own the whole
        screen - a bar under them offers somewhere to go before there is anywhere
        worth going, and the loading screen leaves for Home on its own.
    *@
    @if (!IsFullScreen)
    {
    <nav class="tabbar" role="navigation">
        @foreach (var item in Bottom)
        {
            @if (item.Route == VoiceRoute)
            {
                @* THE LOGO IS THE BUTTON. It is what somebody already presses on
                   Home, so the persistent voice control being the same object
                   means one thing to learn rather than two doing the same job. *@
                @* ONE TURN FROM A PRESS. Listen takes a flag now — the wake word
                   passes it to stay in the conversation — and a button press is
                   already a deliberate act per turn, so it does not. *@
                <button class="tabbar-item tabbar-voice" @onclick="() => Listen()"
                        aria-label="@(Mark.Busy ? "Listening" : "Talk to it")">
                    <span class="voice-ring @(Mark.Busy ? "voice-on" : null)">
                        @* 52 in a 72px ring: the same proportion the mark had at
                           38 in 56, so the button grew without the logo starting
                           to crowd its own edge. *@
                        <BrandMark Size="52" State="Mark.State" Level="Mark.Level" Ring="true"
                                   Decorative="true" />
                    </span>
                    @* The mark is the whole of this button, and a logo is not a
                       name. See .sr-only. *@
                    <span class="sr-only">@(Mark.Busy ? "Listening" : "Talk to it")</span>
                </button>
            }
            else
            {
                <a class="tabbar-item @(IsHere(item.Route) ? "tabbar-on" : null)"
                   href="@item.Route">
                    <span class="tabbar-icon" aria-hidden="true">@((MarkupString)item.Icon)</span>
                    <span class="tabbar-label">@item.Title</span>
                </a>
            }
        }
    </nav>
    }
</div>

@code {
    sealed record BottomItem(string Route, string Title, string Icon);

    /// <summary>What the last turn had to say, or null.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Cleared when the next turn starts and dismissible by tapping it: it is a
    /// reason, not a state, and a reason that will not go away becomes furniture.
    /// </remarks>
    string? _says;



    /// <summary>Five AREAS, not five screens.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// THE FIRST ATTEMPT LISTED THE SCREENS THAT HAPPENED TO EXIST, and got three
    /// of the five wrong for the same reason.
    /// <para>
    /// "Talk" and "Type" were separate items, which made a fallback a peer of the
    /// product: they are one conversation with two input methods, and this
    /// codebase already says so - typing is the BACKUP, the circle is the thing.
    /// Home carries "Or type instead", so nothing is lost by removing it here.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// "CV" was worse. Putting it on the most permanent surface in the app
    /// declares that one of the five things this app IS, is a CV writer - which
    /// is exactly the narrowness the shell was already being blamed for.
    /// Documents is the area; the CV is one thing in it, and CircleAI.Documents
    /// already holds cover letters, invoices and reports for the rest.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// And "Languages" was a once-ever pick given a permanent fifth of the screen.
    /// As VOICE it earns the space: which languages it speaks, what wakes it, and
    /// how to talk to it at all.
    /// </para>
    /// Settings stays last, where a settings item belongs: what you reach for when
    /// something is wrong, not what you came to do.
    /// </remarks>
    // MONOCHROME, currentColor, ONE FAMILY. Emoji cannot be tinted, so the old
    // colour wrench and person stayed grey while the house and gear turned blue
    // on the active tab - a tab bar that only half-responds to where you are.
    // These are the Feather set (MIT): one designer, one grid, one stroke, so the
    // four read as a family, and every stroke inherits currentColor so the whole
    // icon tints with its label. Services is a grid, not a wrench - it opens a
    // grid of tiles, and a grid says that better than a tool did.
    static string Icon(string inner) =>
        "<svg viewBox='0 0 24 24' width='22' height='22' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' " +
        "stroke-width='1.7' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'>" + inner + "</svg>";

    static readonly string HomeIcon = Icon(
        "<path d='M3 9l9-7 9 7v11a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H5a2 2 0 0 1-2-2z'/>" +
        "<polyline points='9 22 9 12 15 12 15 22'/>");
    static readonly string GridIcon = Icon(
        "<rect x='3' y='3' width='7' height='7'/><rect x='14' y='3' width='7' height='7'/>" +
        "<rect x='14' y='14' width='7' height='7'/><rect x='3' y='14' width='7' height='7'/>");
    static readonly string PersonIcon = Icon(
        "<path d='M20 21v-2a4 4 0 0 0-4-4H8a4 4 0 0 0-4 4v2'/><circle cx='12' cy='7' r='4'/>");
    static readonly string GearIcon = Icon(
        "<circle cx='12' cy='12' r='3'/>" +
        "<path d='M19.4 15a1.65 1.65 0 0 0 .33 1.82l.06.06a2 2 0 0 1-2.83 2.83l-.06-.06" +
        "a1.65 1.65 0 0 0-1.82-.33 1.65 1.65 0 0 0-1 1.51V21a2 2 0 0 1-4 0v-.09A1.65 1.65 0 0 0 9 19.4" +
        "a1.65 1.65 0 0 0-1.82.33l-.06.06a2 2 0 0 1-2.83-2.83l.06-.06a1.65 1.65 0 0 0 .33-1.82" +
        "1.65 1.65 0 0 0-1.51-1H3a2 2 0 0 1 0-4h.09A1.65 1.65 0 0 0 4.6 9a1.65 1.65 0 0 0-.33-1.82" +
        "l-.06-.06a2 2 0 0 1 2.83-2.83l.06.06a1.65 1.65 0 0 0 1.82.33H9a1.65 1.65 0 0 0 1-1.51V3" +
        "a2 2 0 0 1 4 0v.09a1.65 1.65 0 0 0 1 1.51 1.65 1.65 0 0 0 1.82-.33l.06-.06a2 2 0 0 1 2.83 2.83" +
        "l-.06.06a1.65 1.65 0 0 0-.33 1.82V9a1.65 1.65 0 0 0 1.51 1H21a2 2 0 0 1 0 4h-.09" +
        "a1.65 1.65 0 0 0-1.51 1z'/>");
    // Two globes would read as the same item, so Translate is the exchange
    // arrows - the sign every translator app uses for "this becomes that" - and
    // Languages keeps the globe on the Services grid.
    static readonly string TranslateIcon = Icon(
        "<polyline points='17 1 21 5 17 9'/><path d='M3 11V9a4 4 0 0 1 4-4h14'/>" +
        "<polyline points='7 23 3 19 7 15'/><path d='M21 13v2a4 4 0 0 1-4 4H3'/>");
    static readonly string ChatIcon = Icon(
        "<path d='M21 15a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H7l-4 4V5a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h14a2 2 0 0 1 2 2z'/>");
    static readonly string VoiceIcon = ""; // the centre is the BrandMark, not a glyph.

    /// <summary>An icon per route. Presentation, so it lives with the markup.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The ROUTES come from AppRoutes; only the glyphs are here. A route with no
    /// icon still renders - it gets an empty span and its label - because a
    /// missing picture is a worse reason to hide a screen than no reason at all.
    /// </remarks>
    static readonly Dictionary<string, string> Icons = new(StringComparer.Ordinal)
    {
        ["home"]      = HomeIcon,
        ["translate"] = TranslateIcon,
        ["chat"]      = ChatIcon,
        ["services"]  = GridIcon,
        ["settings"]  = GearIcon,
        ["you"]       = PersonIcon,
    };

    /// <summary>The bar, built from the one table that owns the routes.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// IT USED TO BE A LIST HERE, and that is how the app came to advertise
    /// translation on its front page while offering no way to reach it: the bar
    /// carried Settings and not the two screens where the work happens. The
    /// note above about Talk and Type is still true about INPUT - one
    /// conversation, two ways in - and it was used to justify removing the only
    /// permanent way to type, on the grounds that Home carries "Or type
    /// instead". A fallback on one screen is not a way in from every screen.
    /// <para>
    /// The voice button is spliced into the middle rather than declared as a
    /// route, because it is not a destination - see VoiceRoute.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    static readonly BottomItem[] Bottom = Build();

    static BottomItem[] Build()
    {
        var items = AppRoutes.TabBar
            .Select(r => new BottomItem(r.Route, r.Title, Icons.GetValueOrDefault(r.Route, "")))
            .ToList();

        // The middle, wherever the middle falls. Five items put it third; four or
        // six put it where the count says, rather than at a hard-coded index that
        // silently moves the microphone when somebody adds a screen.
        items.Insert(items.Count / 2, new BottomItem(VoiceRoute, "Voice", VoiceIcon));
        return [.. items];
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The middle button is the microphone, and it is not a destination.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// IT IS THE PRODUCT, so it sits in the middle where a thumb rests and is
    /// raised above the rest of the bar. Talking is not a screen you go to - it is
    /// what you do, from wherever you already are, which is why it acts in place
    /// rather than navigating. Two items called Talk and Type were the wrong shape
    /// for the same reason: one conversation, reached whatever screen you are on.
    /// </remarks>
    const string VoiceRoute = "#voice";

    /*
        THE NAME COMES FROM THE ROUTE, not from a parameter. Blazor cannot pass a
        value from a routed page up into its layout, and the alternatives -
        cascading the layout instance, or a state service - are plumbing that can
        fall out of step with where you actually are. Reading the path cannot.
    */
    /*
        AND THE NAME COMES FROM AppRoutes, not from a list here.

        This was a hand-written dictionary of eleven screens - the FIFTH copy of
        the app's routes, after the pages, the Services catalogue, the voice
        table and a test's own array. It failed the way every copy of a list
        fails: Transcribe was added, was not added HERE, and the screen rendered
        with no title bar at all. Nothing was broken enough to notice; the app
        just quietly stopped saying where you were.
    */
    /// <summary>The screens that carry no title bar, and why.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Home carries the mark itself and the bottom bar has both, so a bar there
    /// was the same two things twice while taking a fifth of the screen from the
    /// circle. Loading and Not Found are not places you navigated to.
    /// </remarks>
    static readonly string[] Untitled = ["", "home", "not-found"];

    string? ScreenName { get; set; }

    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        Nav.LocationChanged += OnLocationChanged;

        // THE OTHER BUTTON, AND THE WAKE WORD. This mark shows a turn it did not
        // necessarily start - one begun on Home, or by the phone hearing its name
        // with nobody touching it at all - so it has to be told, not just told
        // about its own presses.
        Mark.Changed += OnMarkChanged;

        // AND THE PHONE HEARING ITS OWN NAME. The resident service raises this
        // and, in this head, NOTHING WAS LISTENING FOR IT: the earcon sounded
        // from the service, the wake was real, and then the app did nothing -
        // no turn, no mark, no line. The native head has always answered it
        // (HomeActivity wires HandsFree.Woke the same way); the hybrid was
        // ported without this half.
        //
        // On the layout rather than on Home, because a wake is not about which
        // screen you left open - it works from wherever you already are, which
        // is the whole point of saying the name instead of finding the app.
        Resident.Woke += OnWoke;
        Resolve();

        // AN ADVERT SHARED FROM WHATSAPP LANDS ON THE SCREEN THAT WANTS IT.
        //
        // The activity parks the text and this decides where it goes, because
        // routing belongs here - an activity navigating the web view would be a
        // second router able to disagree with this one. Peeked, not taken: the
        // job screen consumes it, and taking it here would swallow the advert on
        // its way to the only thing that can use it.
        if (Share.HasPending && Path() != "job-spec") Nav.NavigateTo("job-spec");
    }

    void OnLocationChanged(
        object? sender,
        Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Routing.LocationChangedEventArgs e)
    {
        Resolve();

        // AND IT CLEARS WHAT THE VOICE BUTTON SAID. That line is about one turn,
        // taken on one screen - it was living in the layout and following
        // somebody everywhere until the next turn, so "answering needs a 548 MB
        // download" sat under the services list long after it stopped being the
        // thing they were doing, and eventually long after it stopped being true.
        _says = null;

        StateHasChanged();
    }

    string Path()
    {
        var path = Nav.ToBaseRelativePath(Nav.Uri);
        // Drop any query or fragment, or "/languages?q=ja" misses.
        var cut = path.IndexOfAny(['?', '#']);
        if (cut >= 0) path = path[..cut];
        return path.Trim('/');
    }

    void Resolve() => ScreenName =
        Untitled.Contains(Path(), StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase)
            ? null
            : AppRoutes.For(Path())?.Title;

    bool IsHere(string route)
        => string.Equals(Path(), route.Trim('/'), StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase);

    /// <summary>The stages that own the whole screen and carry no bottom bar.</summary>
    bool IsFullScreen => Path() is "" or "setup";

    void GoHome() => Nav.NavigateTo("home");


    /// <summary>One spoken turn, from wherever you already are.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// IN PLACE, NOT A NAVIGATION. Talking is not a screen: sending somebody to a
    /// "voice screen" to say one sentence would throw away whatever they were
    /// looking at, which is usually the thing they are asking about.
    /// <para>
    /// The mark shows the phase - listening scales its arcs with your voice,
    /// thinking travels outward, speaking fires them in sequence - so the button
    /// is also the progress indicator, and there is nowhere else to look.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    /// <summary>
    /// One spoken turn, from wherever you are.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// IT NEVER NAVIGATES. It used to: I made it jump to Chat when it could not
    /// take a turn, to fix a tap that did nothing - and a turn asks for the
    /// microphone, so granting permission dropped somebody onto a different
    /// screen than the one they pressed the button on. Curing silence with a
    /// teleport is worse than the silence.
    /// <para>
    /// This is the one control that works from wherever you already are, so it
    /// has to answer where you already are. It says what happened in a line above
    /// the bar instead: "it needs permission to hear you", "answering needs a
    /// download", "I did not catch that". The reasons already existed - TurnAsync
    /// reports every one of them as a Detail - and this button was throwing them
    /// away, which is why it looked broken rather than blocked.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    /// <summary>The turn this button started, while it is running.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Held so a second press can END it. Null whenever this button is not the
    /// one running a turn - Mark.Busy can be true because the OTHER microphone
    /// owns it, and stopping somebody else's turn from here would be its own
    /// kind of surprise.
    /// </remarks>
    VoiceTurnRouter? _turn;

    /// <summary>How many turns a single wake may carry before it lets go.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// A CONVERSATION, NOT A LOCK-IN. Without a ceiling, a turn that keeps
    /// reporting something heard - a television in the room, a meeting happening
    /// near the phone - would hold the microphone indefinitely, which is the one
    /// behaviour an always-listening assistant must never have.
    /// </remarks>
    const int MaxWakeTurns = 8;

    /// <param name="keepListening">
    /// True when the phone heard its own name: stay in the conversation until
    /// nobody says anything more, instead of taking one turn and going back to
    /// sleep.
    /// </param>
    /// <remarks>
    /// SAYING THE NAME AGAIN FOR EVERY SENTENCE IS NOT A CONVERSATION. A wake
    /// used to buy exactly one turn - ask, get an answer, and the next thing you
    /// say is heard by nothing until you have said "Hey Circle AI" again. Nobody
    /// talks to a person that way.
    /// <para>
    /// So a wake opens a conversation and each turn decides whether there is
    /// another: if a turn hears words, listen again; if it hears nothing,
    /// somebody has stopped talking and the phone goes back to waiting for its
    /// name. The tap button keeps the old single-turn behaviour, because pressing
    /// a button is already a deliberate act per turn.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// A turn that ROUTES ends the conversation too. "Take me to settings" was
    /// answered by going there, and carrying on listening over a screen somebody
    /// has just been moved to would be talking over them.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    async Task Listen(bool keepListening = false)
    {
        // EVEN THIS SAYS SO. A guard that returns in silence is the same dead
        // button from the other side: the mark is mid-turn, the press is correct,
        // and nothing acknowledges it.
        // AND IT NOW SEES THE OTHER BUTTON'S TURN. This tested its own field, so
        // pressing here during a turn started on Home opened a second microphone
        // on top of the first.
        // PRESS AGAIN TO STOP. This used to answer "It is still on the last
        // one." - a refusal dressed as an explanation, while the microphone sat
        // open and somebody had plainly decided they were done. One button, two
        // meanings, and no way to end what you started. The native head settled
        // this long ago: a re-entrant press is STOP.
        if (_turn is { } running)
        {
            running.Stop();
            _says = "Stopped.";
            return;
        }

        if (Mark.Busy)
        {
            _says = "It is still on the last one.";
            return;
        }

        _says = null;

        // WHERE THE TURN IS ALLOWED TO END EARLY. The rule and the wiring both
        // live in one place - see VoiceTurnRouter - because Home needs exactly
        // this and a second copy is how the two marks came to disagree.
        using var router = new VoiceTurnRouter(Can);
        _turn = router;

        // DID THIS TURN HEAR ANYTHING. Set on an INLINE reporter, not through the
        // Progress below: Progress<T> posts to the dispatcher rather than running
        // inline, so a flag set in it is read before it is written - the same
        // property that made a transcript render twice and a cancel land after a
        // delay. See InlineProgress.
        var heard = false;

        IProgress<TurnState> draw = new Progress<TurnState>(t =>
        {
            Mark.Report(t);

            // "I NEED TRANSLATION" USED TO BE ANSWERED RATHER THAN OBEYED: the
            // transcript went straight to the brain, so the app could discuss
            // translating and could not be told to translate.
            router.Observe(t);

            // THE REASON, WHEREVER IT COMES FROM. Every early return in a turn -
            // no model, no permission, nothing heard - arrives as a Detail on an
            // Idle report, and this button used to ignore all of them.
            // NOT AFTER SOMEBODY PRESSED STOP. A cancelled turn still reports its
            // own Detail - "I did not catch that." - which then overwrote the stop
            // message and told somebody the app had failed to hear them, when in
            // fact they had ended it themselves. The turn explanation stops being
            // true the moment ending it was not the turn decision.
            if (!router.StoppedByHand && t.Detail is { Length: > 0 } detail) _says = detail;

            InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
        });

        // The drawing is unchanged and still goes through Progress; only the flag
        // is read inline, so this cannot alter how anything renders.
        var updates = new InlineProgress<TurnState>(t =>
        {
            if (t.Heard is { Length: > 0 }) heard = true;
            draw.Report(t);
        });

        try
        {
            for (var taken = 0; taken < MaxWakeTurns; taken++)
            {
                heard = false;
                await Talk.TurnAsync(updates, router.Token);

                // ONE TURN UNLESS THE PHONE STARTED IT. Pressing the button is a
                // deliberate act per turn; hearing its own name is an opening.
                if (!keepListening) break;

                // NOBODY SAID ANYTHING, so the conversation is over - which is
                // the same signal a person uses, and needs no timer of its own.
                if (!heard) break;

                // Stopped by hand, cancelled, or routed somewhere: all three mean
                // this is no longer a conversation to carry on.
                if (router.StoppedByHand || router.Token.IsCancellationRequested
                    || router.Routed is not null) break;
            }
        }
        catch (Exception ex) when (router.Ended(ex)) { /* routed, not failed */ }
        catch (Exception ex) { _says = $"{ex.GetType().Name}: {ex.Message}"; }
        finally { _turn = null; Mark.Clear(); StateHasChanged(); }

        await router.GoAsync(Nav, line => Talk.SayAsync(line), line => { _says = line; StateHasChanged(); });
    }

    /// <summary>Put the assistant back on, if that is how it was left.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// ON FIRST RENDER, WHICH IS THE POINT: starting a foreground service is
    /// only allowed from a visible screen, and by the time this runs there is
    /// one. ResumeAsync itself decides whether anything should start - it reads
    /// the owner's recorded choice and returns Off without touching the
    /// microphone when the answer is no.
    /// <para>
    /// Not awaited into the render path. Bringing the service up loads a wake
    /// bundle, and a layout that waits for it is a layout that has not drawn
    /// yet; the toggle in Settings reads IsListening live, so it catches up on
    /// its own.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (!firstRender) return;

        try { await Resident.ResumeAsync(); }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            // Never fatal to the shell. An assistant that could not restart is a
            // reason to say so, not a reason for the app to fail to draw.
            _says = $"Could not start listening: {ex.Message}";
            StateHasChanged();
        }
    }

    /// <summary>The phone heard its name, so take the turn nobody pressed for.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// ARRIVES OFF THE UI THREAD - the wake detector raises it from its own
    /// audio loop - so everything here goes through InvokeAsync. Listen already
    /// refuses a second turn and says so, which is the behaviour wanted: a wake
    /// in the middle of a turn must not open a second microphone, and the native
    /// head learned the hard way that dropping it in SILENCE makes a stuck turn
    /// look like a phone that beeps at its name and then ignores you.
    /// </remarks>
    /// <remarks>
    /// KEEPS LISTENING, because the phone started this one. Saying the name
    /// again for every sentence is not a conversation - see Listen.
    /// </remarks>
    void OnWoke(object? sender, string phrase) =>
        InvokeAsync(() => Listen(keepListening: true));

    // OFF THE UI THREAD. A turn reports from the audio loop and the wake word
    // from a background service, so this cannot touch component state directly.
    void OnMarkChanged() => InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Nav.LocationChanged -= OnLocationChanged;
        Mark.Changed -= OnMarkChanged;
        Resident.Woke -= OnWoke;
    }
}
